oth epics' scope and acceptance criteria state the image is\n\"built from the midstream source\" — the midstream repository that the\nrepo-onboarding epic creates. The productization work (Dockerfile, base-image and\nlabeling conventions, version pinning) is authored in and built from that\nrepository; it cannot start or complete before the repo exists. The decomposition\nitself establishes the principle for the sibling edge (E006 → E008, \"konflux\nonboarding requires the repo to exist; always serial\") but omits the identical\ndependency for the image-build epic. This is discoverable directly from epic\ncontent (scope and ACs of E009/E013) and means E009/E013 can be scheduled before\nthe midstream repo exists — incorrect execution. Required fix: add\nBRANCH-A-E006 → BRANCH-A-E009 and BRANCH-B-E010 → BRANCH-B-E013. (Note this does\nnot conflict with Rule 10: repo-onboarding vs general implementation is not a\nmandated-parallel pairing; only konflux-onboarding vs general implementation is.)\n\nEverything else in the DAG is correct:\n\n- No circular dependencies; no invalid edges (all referenced epics exist).\n- Frontmatter `dependencies` match the summary DAG diagrams exactly, in both the\n  main plan and both branches, and arrows run dependency → dependent.\n- Mandated parallelism respected: no konflux-onboarding → general implementation\n  edge (BRANCH-A-E008 ∥ BRANCH-A-E009; BRANCH-B-E012 ∥ BRANCH-B-E013, with the\n  Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC as the only coupling); no\n  license-validation → repo-onboarding or license-validation → konflux-onboarding\n  edges. Both konflux epics checked against every general implementation epic\n  (E002, E003, E004, E009, E013) — no forbidden edges.\n- The image digest consumed by E002 is correctly handled as a configuration\n  reference (AC gate on E002, not a dependency edge), and no main-plan epic lists\n  a branch epic in its dependencies.\n- All 8 branch files set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by`\n  (E001), and every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct dependencies.\n- Critical path length 3 (main plan) is reasonable for an M-size strategy; the\n  branch chains are length 3, within the 4-5 heuristic for a new component even\n  after the missing edge is added.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEach epic is a single component/team tuple. E003 (Data Science Pipelines / RHOAI\nPipelines) and E004 (Serving Runtimes / RHOAI Model Serving) are correctly\nseparated. No scope duplication: the branch A / branch B chains target different\nupstreams under a mutually exclusive conditional — only one executes. No\nwork-product type mixing: docs authoring is isolated in E005. Epic sizes are\nsprint-sized; E002 is the largest but remains a coherent single-sprint operator\nchange.\n\nMinor (does not reduce score): E001 carries `component: Platform Operator` with\n`team: Legal` — an attribution convenience for an Investigation that performs no\ncomponent work; acceptable given the component-name constraint but slightly\nimprecise.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (AGPL cleared vs not cleared)\nselects between branch A and branch B, changing which downstream epics exist.\nAll downstream epics of E001 correctly set `gated_by: E001` with\n`gate_failure_impact` populated with both `action` and `fallback_approach`\n(rewrite for E002, add_remediation for E003/E004, remove for branch epics — all\ndescribe real changes on gate failure). The E003/E004 gates are justified by\nHLR-1's explicit \"blocks all shipping work\" language, and their fallback\n(retarget endpoint wiring) is a real scope change, not a bare scheduling\ndependency. E005 has no gate and correctly so. All Implementation epics produce\nconcrete artifacts.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout: E001 carries the 5-signal\n`investigation_signals` rubric; all 12 Implementation epics carry the 9-signal\n`ai_signals` rubric. All 13 ai-signals rationale files exist and their values\nmatch the frontmatter. Spot-checked against architecture context: E003's\n`architecture_claims: 1` is backed by the Data Science Pipelines context file\n(which documents S3-compatible artifact storage), and E004's\n`architecture_claims: -1` is accurate — no architecture-context file exists for\nServing Runtimes or KServe. E001's `human_judgment_required: -2` is correct for a\nlegal/governance decision. Arguable but defensible: E003/E004 score\n`open_questions: 0` while E002 scores `-1` for the same upstream uncertainty —\nthe differentiation (deployment rewrite vs a configuration value) is reasoned in\nthe rationales and is not a rubric contradiction.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy.\nRule-mandated ACs are present everywhere they apply: E005 has the technical\nreview AC; E009 and E013 both carry the Rule 25 \"Konflux build pipeline green\"\nAC (as an AC, not a DAG edge — correct); E002 has the build-pipeline-green AC\nplus an opt-in/no-regression AC covering the feature-flag concern for the new\nenablement; E003/E004 have no-regression ACs for customer-provided storage. No\nepic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: the four HLRs, the out-of-scope boundaries\n(explicitly an AC on E005), and the open question on the Apache-2.0 fallback —\ncovered by branch B, with the tentative-candidate risk correctly handled\n(confirming the fallback upstream is the first step of BRANCH-B-E010). The two\nbounded legal outcomes map one-to-one onto branches A and B. Dashboard, listed in\nAffected Components with no corresponding HLR, is handled as a passive component\nwith validation ACs on E003 and E004 rather than a spurious epic. Strategy-level\ncontext is carried forward: the carried-fork maintenance burden and the\ncomponents-vs-HLR mismatch are both