he branch productization chains (BRANCH-A-E006..E009,\n45\t  BRANCH-B-E010..E013).\n46\t- HLR-3 (P1) → E003 (pipelines portion) and E004 (model serving portion).\n47\t- HLR-4 (P2) → E005 (covered, not deferred).\n48\t\n49\tPriority inheritance is correct: E002/E003/E004 and all branch epics depend only\n50\ton the P0 Investigation E001. E005 is a docs-authoring epic; its P1 priority\n51\tcorrectly derives from the strategy's `priority: Major` via the docs-authoring\n52\texception, not from the P2 HLR-4. No priority collapse: E003/E004 split HLR-3\n53\talong component/team lines so the P1 work is independently plannable; the branch\n54\tepics are mutually exclusive conditionals, not bundled deferrable features.\n55\t\n56\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n57\t\n58\t**Critical — missing data/artifact dependency edges in both branches.**\n59\tBRANCH-A-E009 (productized MinIO image) and BRANCH-B-E013 (productized\n60\tfallback-store image) have `dependencies` of only [E001, branch license-validation\n61\tepic], with no edge from the branch repo-onboarding epic (BRANCH-A-E006 /\n62\tBRANCH-B-E010). Yet both epics' scope and acceptance criteria state the image is\n63\t\"built from the midstream source\" — the midstream repository that the\n64\trepo-onboarding epic creates. The productization work (Dockerfile, base-image and\n65\tlabeling conventions, version pinning) is authored in and built from that\n66\trepository; it cannot start or complete before the repo exists. The decomposition\n67\titself establishes the principle for the sibling edge (E006 → E008, \"konflux\n68\tonboarding requires the repo to exist; always serial\") but omits the identical\n69\tdependency for the image-build epic. This is discoverable directly from epic\n70\tcontent (scope and ACs of E009/E013) and means E009/E013 can be scheduled before\n71\tthe midstream repo exists — incorrect execution. Required fix: add\n72\tBRANCH-A-E006 → BRANCH-A-E009 and BRANCH-B-E010 → BRANCH-B-E013. (Note this does\n73\tnot conflict with Rule 10: repo-onboarding vs general implementation is not a\n74\tmandated-parallel pairing; only konflux-onboarding vs general implementation is.)\n75\t\n76\tEverything else in the DAG is correct:\n77\t\n78\t- No circular dependencies; no invalid edges (all referenced epics exist).\n79\t- Frontmatter `dependencies` match the summary DAG diagrams exactly, in both the\n80\t  main plan and both branches, and arrows run dependency → dependent.\n81\t- Mandated parallelism respected: no konflux-onboarding → general implementation\n82\t  edge (BRANCH-A-E008 ∥ BRANCH-A-E009; BRANCH-B-E012 ∥ BRANCH-B-E013, with the\n83\t  Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC as the only coupling); no\n84\t  license-validation → repo-onboarding or license-validation → konflux-onboarding\n85\t  edges. Both konflux epics checked against every general implementation epic\n86\t  (E002, E003, E004, E009, E013) — no forbidden edges.\n87\t- The image digest consumed by E002 is correctly handled as a configuration\n88\t  reference (AC gate on E002, not a dependency edge), and no main-plan epic lists\n89\t  a branch epic in its dependencies.\n90\t- All 8 branch files set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by`\n91\t  (E001), and every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct dependencies.\n92\t- Critical path length 3 (main plan) is reasonable for an M-size strategy; the\n93\t  branch chains are length 3, within the 4-5 heuristic for a new component even\n94\t  after the missing edge is added.\n95\t\n96\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n97\t\n98\tEach epic is a single component/team tuple. E003 (Data Science Pipelines / RHOAI\n99\tPipelines) and E004 (Serving Runtimes / RHOAI Model Serving) are correctly\n100\tseparated. No scope duplication: the branch A / branch B chains target different\n101\tupstreams under a mutually exclusive conditional — only one executes. No\n102\twork-product type mixing: docs authoring is isolated in E005. Epic sizes are\n103\tsprint-sized; E002 is the largest but remains a coherent single-sprint operator\n104\tchange.\n105\t\n106\tMinor (does not reduce score): E001 carries `component: Platform Operator` with\n107\t`team: Legal` — an attribution convenience for an Investigation that performs no\n108\tcomponent work; acceptable given the component-name constraint but slightly\n109\timprecise.\n110\t\n111\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n112\t\n113\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (AGPL cleared vs not cleared)\n114\tselects between branch A and branch B, changing which downstream epics exist.\n115\tAll downstream epics of E001 correctly set `gated_by: E001` with\n116\t`gate_failure_impact` populated with both `action` and `fallback_approach`\n117\t(rewrite for E002, add_remediation for E003/E004, remove for branch epics — all\n118\tdescribe real changes on gate failure). The E003/E004 gates are justified by\n119\tHLR-1's explicit \"blocks all shipping work\" language, and their fallback\n120\t(retarget endpoint wiring) is a real scope change, not a bare scheduling\n121\tdependency. E005 has no gate and correctly so. All Implementation epics produce\n122\tconcrete artifacts.\n123\t\n124\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n125\t\n126\tType-appropriate signal fields are used throughout: E001 carries the 5-signal\n127\t`investigation_signals` rubric; all 12 Implementation epics carry the 9-signal\n128\t`ai_signals` rubric. All 13 ai-signals rationale files exist and their values\n129\tmatch the frontmatter. Spot-checked against architecture context: E003's\n130\t`architecture_claims: 1` is backed by the Data Science Pipelines context file\n131\t(which documents S3-compatible artifact storage), and E004's\n132\t`architecture_claims: -1` is accurate — no architecture-context file exists for\n133\tServing Runtimes or KServe. E001's `human_judgment_required: -2` is correct for a\n134\tlegal/governance decision. Arguable but defensible: E003/E004 score\n135\t`open_questions: 0` while E002 scores `-1` for the same upstream uncertainty —\n136\tthe differentiation (deployment rewrite vs a configuration value) is reasoned in\n137\tthe rationales and is not a rubric contradiction.\n138\t\n139\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n140\t\n141\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy.\n142\tRule-mandated ACs are present everywhere they apply: E005 has the technical\n143\treview AC; E009 and E013 both carry the Rule 25 \"Konflux build pipeline green\"\n144\tAC (as an AC, not a DAG edge — correct); E002 has the build-pipeline-green AC\n145\tplus an opt-in/no-regression AC covering the feature-flag concern for the new\n146\tenablement; E003/E004 have no-regression ACs for customer-provided storage. No\n147\tepic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n148\t\n149\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n150\t\n151\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: the four HLRs, the out-of-scope boundaries\n152\t(explicitly an AC on E005), and the open question on the Apache-2.0 fallback —\n153\tcovered by branch B, with the tentative-candidate risk correctly handled\n154\t(confirming the fallback upstream is the first step of BRANCH-B-E010). The two\n155\tbounded legal outcomes map one-to-one onto branches A and B. Dashboard, listed in\n156\tAffected Components with no corresponding HLR, is handled as a passive component\n157\twith validation ACs on E003 and E004 rather than a spurious epic. Strategy-level\n158\tcontext is carried forward: the carried-fork maintenance burden and the\n159\tcomponents-vs-HLR mismatch are both flagged as health warnings rather than\n160\tsilently dropped.\n161\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8db1aca161d32892","agent_id":"ac67f86ba196777aa","session_id":"fb56929f-df9e-42c6-b5d2-389b8632f829","uuid":"e6384a3e-70bb-4cef-966d-ce87343dbfb3","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:28:23.288Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-97a92d48e7b3432e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"