l productization chains in both branches\n44\t  (E006-E009 / E010-E013).\n45\t- HLR-3 (P1): E003 (pipelines portion) and E004 (model serving portion).\n46\t- HLR-4 (P2): E005.\n47\t\n48\tThe traceability matrix is present, and every epic carries an HLR Traceability\n49\tsection. Priority inheritance is correct: E001, the epic blocking all P0 work,\n50\tis itself P0; branch chain epics are P0; E003/E004 (P1) are enabled by a P0\n51\tgate. E005's P1 priority is correct under the docs-authoring exception\n52\t(strategy priority Major → P1), and its P2-mapped HLR does not propagate\n53\tupstream. No priority collapse: no epic bundles deferrable lower-priority\n54\tfeatures with P0 work.\n55\t\n56\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n57\t\n58\t**Critical — frontmatter dependencies inconsistent with the branch DAG\n59\tdiagrams.** BRANCH-A-E008's frontmatter lists `RHAISTRAT-9012-E001` in\n60\t`dependencies` (required, since `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9012-E001` must reference\n61\ta direct dependency), but the Branch A DAG diagram in the decomposition summary\n62\tomits the E001 → BRANCH-A-E008 edge — it shows only A006 → A008. The same\n63\tomission exists in Branch B: BRANCH-B-E012 depends on E001 in frontmatter, but\n64\tthe Branch B diagram shows only B010 → B012. The diagrams are internally\n65\tinconsistent about which gated edges they render (E001 → E006/E007/E009 are\n66\tdrawn; the identical gate on the Konflux epics is not), so the diagrams\n67\tmisrepresent the recorded dependency structure. The main-plan diagram matches\n68\tfrontmatter exactly; the defect is confined to the two branch diagrams. Fix by\n69\tadding the E001 → BRANCH-A-E008 and E001 → BRANCH-B-E012 edges to the branch\n70\tDAG diagrams (the frontmatter side is correct as-is).\n71\t\n72\tEverything else in the DAG is sound:\n73\t\n74\t- No circular dependencies; no edge references a nonexistent epic.\n75\t- Implementation-type parallelism verified pairwise across all epics:\n76\t  konflux-onboarding (BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-B-E012) has no blocking edge in\n77\t  either direction with any general implementation epic (BRANCH-A-E009,\n78\t  BRANCH-B-E013, E002, E003, E004) — Rule 10 is enforced via the\n79\t  \"build pipeline green\" AC on the productization epics, as required.\n80\t  license-validation (E007/E011) is parallel to both repo-onboarding (Rule 7)\n81\t  and konflux-onboarding (Rule 8); license-validation → productization is\n82\t  correctly serial (Rule 9).\n83\t- The repo-onboarding → productization edge (E006→E009, E010→E013) is a\n84\t  legitimate Rule 4 data/artifact dependency (build recipe authored in the\n85\t  midstream repo), not a Rule 10 violation.\n86\t- The productized image digest consumed by E002 is correctly handled as a\n87\t  configuration-reference AC (\"pinned image digest available at wiring time\"),\n88\t  not a dependencies edge.\n89\t- No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies; all eight branch\n90\t  files set both `branch` and `gated_by` correctly.\n91\t- Critical path length 3 is reasonable for the strategy size given the heavy\n92\t  parallelism (branch chains excluded from main-plan critical path by design).\n93\t\n94\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n95\t\n96\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: Legal (E001), Platform Operator\n97\t(E002 + branch chains), RHOAI Pipelines (E003), RHOAI Model Serving (E004),\n98\tRHOAI Docs (E005). All components used are canonical per\n99\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` (including \"Serving Runtimes\" for E004). The\n100\tbranch A/B chains duplicate structure but are mutually exclusive conditional\n101\toutcomes, not scope duplication. No epic bundles code with content authoring\n102\t(E005 is pure docs-authoring). No epic exceeds ~2 weeks of work.\n103\t\n104\tMinor (does not affect score): E001 carries `component: Platform Operator` as\n105\ta documented attribution convenience while performing no component work —\n106\tacceptable given the schema requires a component and no canonical component\n107\tcovers legal work, but worth revisiting if the schema ever allows null.\n108\t\n109\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n110\t\n111\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (AGPL cleared vs. not) selects\n112\tbetween Branch A and Branch B, changing which downstream epics exist. All\n113\tImplementation epics produce concrete artifacts (operator capability, config\n114\tdefaults, docs, repo, scan report, pipeline, image). Every epic with a\n115\tnon-null `gated_by` has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and\n116\t`fallback_approach` populated and meaningful (rewrite / add_remediation /\n117\tremove). Every epic listing the Investigation E001 in `dependencies` has\n118\t`gated_by` set. Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct\n119\t`dependencies` list. E003/E004's gate is a true gate (the strategy's HLR-1\n120\tblocks all shipping work; the fallback approach retargets endpoint wiring),\n121\tnot a mere scheduling dependency.\n122\t\n123\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n124\t\n125\tSignal sets are type-appropriate everywhere: E001 carries\n126\t`investigation_signals`; all twelve Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`.\n127\tSignal values were verified against the strategy content and the architecture\n128\tcontext:\n129\t\n130\t- E001's `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the answer is a\n131\t  pending human legal decision; `human_judgment_required: -2` is justified.\n132\t- E003's `architecture_claims: +1` and `change_specificity: +1` are backed by\n133\t  the DSP architecture-context file, which documents S3-compatible object\n134\t  storage for artifacts.\n135\t- E004's `architecture_claims: -1` is correct — no architecture-context file\n136\t  exists for serving components (only Dashboard and Data Science Pipelines\n137\t  have context files).\n138\t- Branch konflux epics' `pattern_precedent: +1` is backed by Konflux\n139\t  mentions in both existing context files.\n140\t- E010/E013's `open_que