| Rules 13, 10 | The productized image digest consumed by the operator is a configuration reference: E002 gets a \"pinned image digest available\" AC, not a dependency. Also, main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics. |\n103\t\n104\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n105\t\n106\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n107\t|-----|----------|-------|\n108\t| HLR-1 (P0): Legal review of redistributing MinIO (AGPL-3.0), documented, incl. derivative-work posture | P0 | E001 |\n109\t| HLR-2 (P0): Admin-enableable bundled S3 store via Platform Operator, productized images, upgrade lifecycle | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A-E006..E009, BRANCH-B-E010..E013 |\n110\t| HLR-3 (P1): DSP and model serving connection defaults target the bundled store | P1 | E003, E004 |\n111\t| HLR-4 (P2): Documentation (enablement, sizing, supported/unsupported boundary) | P2 | E005 |\n112\t\n113\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n114\t\n115\tNote on E005 priority: HLR-4 is P2, but the docs-authoring priority exception derives\n116\tpriority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Major` → P1). Docs priority does not\n117\tpropagate to upstream implementations.\n118\t\n119\t## Component Mapping Notes\n120\t\n121\t- The bundled S3-compatible object store (MinIO / SeaweedFS) is not in\n122\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt` and has no architecture-context file — it is a new\n123\t  component delivered through the Platform Operator. Per the component-name constraint,\n124\t  all store-related epics use the closest canonical component: **Platform Operator**.\n125\t- E001 (Legal investigation) carries `component: Platform Operator` with `team: Legal`.\n126\t  This is an attribution convenience only: the epic performs no component work — its\n127\t  deliverable is a legal decision record. The component field is required by the schema\n128\t  and no canonical component corresponds to legal work, so the closest canonical\n129\t  component to the investigation's subject (the operator-bundled store) is used.\n130\t- Affected Components lists \"Dashboard\", but no HLR or described change covers a\n131\t  Dashboard code change. Dashboard is treated as a **passive component**: validation that\n132\t  Dashboard views continue to function against the bundled store is an acceptance\n133\t  criterion on E003 (pipelines views) and E004 (serving views) — the nearest active\n134\t  components. No separate Dashboard epic is generated.\n135\t- HLR-3 explicitly covers \"model serving connection defaults\", but no serving component\n136\t  appears in Affected Components. E004 was created for full HLR coverage using the\n137\t  parent-level canonical component \"Serving Runtimes\" (no architecture-context file\n138\t  exists for KServe or Serving Runtimes). Flagged as a health warning below.\n139\t\n140\t## Health Warnings\n141\t\n142\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams\n143\t   table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n144\t   Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments were inferred (RHOAI Platform Operator,\n145\t   RHOAI Pipelines per architecture context, RHOAI Model Serving, RHOAI Docs, Legal).\n146\t   Per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text.\n147\t2. **Affected Components vs HLR mismatch**: Dashboard is listed as affected but has no\n148\t   corresponding requirement; model serving has a requirement (HLR-3) but is not listed\n149\t   as affected. Treated as passive and added respectively (see Component Mapping Notes).\n150\t3. **Scope trap — carried-fork support burden**: \"productized images and upgrade\n151\t   lifecycle\" implies ongoing CVE/patch maintenance of a midstream fork of the selected\n152\t   upstream. The strategy does not budget this; the productization branch epics carry an\n153\t   upgrade-path acceptance criterion, but long-term maintenance ownership is unassigned.\n154\t4. **Branch B candidate is tentative**: the strategy names SeaweedFS only as an example\n155\t   (\"e.g. SeaweedFS\"). If the legal review forces the fallback, the specific Apache-2.0\n156\t   upstream should be confirmed as the first step of Branch B (noted in BRANCH-B-E010);\n157\t   this does not change the epic structure.\n158\t\n159\t## Tiered Delivery\n160\t\n161\tNot applied as a Tier 1/Tier 2 split on external dependency timing. The upstream is\n162\tconsumed, not contributed to (no upstream PR/RFC), so Rule 21 does not generate an\n163\tupstream Implementation epic. The uncertainty here is legal, handled by the E001\n164\tInvestigation with conditional branches: Branch A (MinIO cleared) and Branch B\n165\t(Apache-2.0 fallback, restarts image productization). No partial-value tier is possible\n166\tbefore clearance because HLR-1 blocks all shipping work.\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ddb14dffb998325","agent_id":"abad1c67621acda9b","session_id":"fb56929f-df9e-42c6-b5d2-389b8632f829","uuid":"a3cd5996-c7c1-4481-8de0-ce862350004e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:36:07.691Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-82dda08b1b2c6f58","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Final review RHAISTRAT-9012"}
